Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; softly painted period nursery and woodland, with all characters rendered in one cohesive storybook style.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT with spotted brown-and-white velveteen, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, and a holly sprig between his paws sits atop a stocking.

(SOUND: paper rustles, a clockwork mouse ticks, children laugh downstairs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

A small BOY lifts the Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him against his velvet dressing gown.

(SOUND: a toy engine whirs across the nursery floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

The nursery door opens. Wrapped parcels pile onto the carpet. The Boy drops the Rabbit beside the toy cupboard and rushes to the new presents.

(SOUND: excited voices and tearing tissue paper)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight falls over scattered toys. The Rabbit lies near the fender beside the SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy horse bald in patches, seams showing, tail nearly plucked bare.

(SOUND: fire crackles softly in the grate)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and pretended they were real. The only person kind to the Rabbit was the Skin Horse.

The Rabbit turns his boot-button eyes toward the Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

The Skin Horse’s worn glass eye catches the firelight.

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

(SOUND: a floorboard creaks in the hall)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

SKIN HORSE
By the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and you get very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA, a brisk woman in a black dress and white apron, searches the bedclothes while the Boy waits in his nightshirt. The Rabbit hangs halfway from the open toy cupboard.

(SOUND: wind taps the windowpane and a mantel clock ticks)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She pulls the Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy’s arms.

The Boy folds the Rabbit beneath his chin.

(SOUND: bedsprings creak as the Boy settles)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy makes a tunnel under the blanket and pushes the Rabbit through it.

BOY
This is like the burrows the real rabbits live in.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy used to talk to him, and made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes.

The Boy’s hand closes around the Rabbit all night. The Rabbit’s pink nose rubs against the Boy’s cheek.

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

The Rabbit sits on the floor, shabbier now: his tail hangs loose, the pink has rubbed from his nose, and his thread whiskers have thinned.

(SOUND: Nana sweeps bristles across the nursery rug)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

Nana swoops toys into the cupboard. The Rabbit lands softly on a cushion.

(SOUND: tin toys clatter inside the cupboard)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Wherever he was thrown, he came down soft.

EXT. GARDEN - DUSK

The Boy’s wheelbarrow stands beside a flower bed. The Rabbit, damp with dew and earthy from a small burrow, lies beneath raspberry canes.

(SOUND: evening birds call and leaves stir in a light wind)

Nana approaches with a candle lantern and finds him.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sits upright in bed, reaching toward Nana. The damp Rabbit rests in her apron.

(SOUND: rain begins against the glass)

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana places the Rabbit into the Boy’s hands. The Boy presses his face to the Rabbit’s faded nose.

(SOUND: the rain deepens, then softens)

NARRATOR (V.O)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

NARRATOR (V.O)
The nursery magic had happened to him.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SUMMER EVENING

In a nest of bracken, the Rabbit is now very worn: greyed pink ears, faded brown spots, loose tail, and only a few thread whiskers. Ants march between his velvet paws.

(SOUND: insects hum and distant children play among the trees)

The Boy sets him down gently.

BOY
You will be quite cosy here, Bunny.

The Boy runs away among the trees. Two WILD RABBITS, sleek and furry, creep from the bracken and circle the Rabbit.

(SOUND: soft pawsteps through dry fern)

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

The Wild Rabbit gives a high sideways hop and rises on hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit remains still, his rounded velveteen back pressed into the grass.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

The Wild Rabbit dances in a circle, then leans close. His whiskers brush the Rabbit’s ear. He jerks back.

(SOUND: bracken snaps as the wild rabbits recoil)

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy’s footsteps approach. The Wild Rabbits flash away through the ferns.

(SOUND: quick thumps of rabbit feet fading into the wood)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back!

The Rabbit lies alone while the bracken sways.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SPRING DAY

The Boy, pale and thin after illness, plays slowly among the bracken. His old velvet dressing jacket hangs loosely on his small frame.

(SOUND: warm wind moves through leaves and birds sing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weeks passed, and the little Rabbit grew very old and shabby, but the Boy loved him just as much.

The Boy lifts the Rabbit, kisses his worn nose, and carries him home.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy burns with scarlet fever beneath white bedclothes. The Rabbit is hidden against him, flattened and threadbare, his faded ears and loose tail trapped under the Boy’s hand.

(SOUND: the Boy’s strained breathing and a glass clinking on a bedside tray)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went in the nursery, and a light burned all night.

The Rabbit remains beneath the blanket as the Boy tosses and murmurs.

BOY
Bunny...

(SOUND: a feverish cough, then the low hiss of the night-light)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away.

The Boy’s fever breaks. Morning sun reaches the pillow as he opens his eyes and pulls the Rabbit close.

(SOUND: birds begin outside the open window)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And presently the fever turned, and the Boy got better.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The recovered Boy sits wrapped in a shawl beside open windows. A DOCTOR in a dark coat examines the room while Nana gathers books and toys. The worn Rabbit peeps from the bedclothes.

(SOUND: gulls cry faintly beyond the house and curtains flutter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside. The room was to be disinfected, and all the books and toys he had played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana notices the Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

The Doctor looks at the ragged velveteen toy.

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

BOY
Bunny goes to the seaside.

DOCTOR
Nonsense. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more.

(SOUND: Nana closes a sack with a rough pull of cord)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

An untied sack lies beside old picture-books and garden rubbish. The worn Rabbit wriggles his head through its opening. Raspberry canes rise behind him under moonlight.

(SOUND: chickens rustle inside the fowl-house and cold wind hisses through canes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The little Rabbit lay among the old picture-books in the corner behind the fowl-house, and he felt very lonely.

The Rabbit looks toward the garden where fairy huts once stood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear gathers on his shabby velvet nose and falls into the earth.

(SOUND: the tear drops onto dry soil)

A flower pushes up where the tear fell: emerald leaves, then a golden cup-shaped blossom.

(SOUND: a faint bell-like chime rises from the flower)

The blossom opens. The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps out, small and luminous, dressed in pearl, dew-drops, and flowers.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
No.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

The Fairy gathers the Rabbit in her arms and kisses his damp nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. WOODLAND GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

Silver bracken surrounds a velvet-green glade. Wild Rabbits dance in a ring, then stop as the Fairy arrives carrying the threadbare Velveteen Rabbit.

(SOUND: night insects pulse beneath a soft chiming melody)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land.

The Fairy sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits frozen, looking at the other rabbits.

(SOUND: wild rabbits sniff and shuffle through the grass)

Something tickles his nose. He raises a hind foot to scratch it. He stops.

His velveteen has become soft brown fur; long whiskers brush the grass; strong hind legs bend beneath him. Faint old spots show through his new coat.

(SOUND: a bright musical swell and one delighted rabbit thump)

The Rabbit leaps, springs sideways, and whirls in the moonlit grass with the others.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SPRING DAY

A year later, the Boy runs through the warm wood, healthy again in his velvet jacket. Two rabbits peep from the bracken: one is brown, and the other has faint, strange spots beneath his fur.

(SOUND: birdsong, breeze, and soft rabbit feet in leaves)

The spotted Rabbit watches the Boy with round black eyes.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Rabbit twitches his long whiskers and disappears gently into the bracken.

(SOUND: a final soft thump of hind feet, fading into birdsong)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.

THE END
